    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The feature extractor can be implemented using any kind of GNNs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph Neural Networks. The early GNNs generalize convolution nets to graphs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">We formally characterize how expressive different GNN variants are in learning to represent and distinguish between di</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Despite the recent success of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), training GNNs on large graphs remains challenging.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Recent approaches leverage high-order information from graph structure with GNNs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Such graph sparsity inherently limits the effective information propagation and the performance of GNNs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">it causes the GNN to become a predictor for y and not y</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Over-smoothing stops GNNs from going deep. FLAG shows its ability to boost both shallow and deep baselines, e.g., GCN and DeeperGCN.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Despite the empirical success of GNNs, there has been relatively little work that mathematically studies their properties.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">a shared GNN model denoted as GNN (this could be any GNN model that works for the graph classication task)</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs effectively learn node representations using two components in graph data</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A GNN learns a representation for every node in the graph using a set of stacked graph convolution layers.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph neural networks (GNNs) have recently achieved state-of-the-art performance in many graph-based applications. Despite the high performance, they typically need to perform expensive recursive neighborhood expansions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph neural networks (GNNs) have achieved state-of-the-art performance of node classification.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs dealing with non-grid data within a discrete space</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">methods based on graph neural networks (GNNs) [ 7 , 11 , 29 ] have demonstrated their effectiveness in classifying node labels.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs can take nongird data as inputs. A non-grid data can be represented as a graph G = { V, E } and a set of features h</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs leverage graph structure, which in practice, allows them to make more accurate predictions</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the performance degradation caused by large in deep GNNs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs dealing with non-grid data within a discrete space.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">it allows to use any GNN architecture with any Aggregate function for the backbone model</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), which can learn effective representations from irregular data</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">we implement it with GNN, and train it on two down-stream tasks, edge prediction and node classification.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">the total latency explodes quickly as L increases. Figure 1 shows the dependency added by each GNN layer and the exponential explosion of inference time.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs scale to large graphs by minibatch training and subsample node neighbors to deal with high degree nodes.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph Neural Networks (GNNs), which can learn effective representations from irregular data, have given excellent performances in various graphbased tasks</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Common inference acceleration techniques like pruning ( Zhou et al. , 2021 ) and quantization ( Tailor et al. , 2021 ; Zhao et al. , 2020 ) can speed up GNNs</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">we propose a universal GNN framework that tackles both the unsupervised graph embedding and the semi-supervised node classification tasks.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are the rst choice methods for graph machine learning problems thanks to their ability to learn state-of-the-art level representations from graph-structured data.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">newer GNN architectures are getting deeper from 64 layers ( Chen et al. , 2020 ) to even 1001 layers</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">The recent success of Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) [ 53 ] has boosted</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Modern GNNs typically adopt a neighborhood aggregation strategy by using 2322 the features and graph structures to learn discriminative node or graph representations.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs effectively learn node representations using two components in graph data: they process node features through distributed node-wise transformation</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">common GNNs alternately stack graph convolutions, lin</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">GNNs in learning node representations from the local neighborhood</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) are the predominant technique for learning over graphs.</hasEvidenceText>
    <hasEvidenceText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">mainstream GNNs cannot be more powerful than the 1-Weisfeiler-Lehman (WL) isomorphism test</hasEvidenceText>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://example.org/S0/R1#compressionmethod">
